export const OPENERS_SMALL_B = {
  "small-animals/french-lop": [
    "The French lop is a large, heavy lop rabbit, and it is not a dwarf lop. Size on the card is large, 10-15 lbs, and the lifespan is 5-7 years. Ears and weight are the welfare issues that come with that body. The ears hang against the bedding, so the canals stay warm and damp, and a rabbit in that weight range loads the hocks, the spine, and the joints every time it sits. Care level is intermediate. Activity is moderate. Social needs are social. None of those lines turn a French lop into a small lop that can live in a hutch built for a dwarf.",
    "Space on the card says only large enclosure. For a 10-15 lb rabbit that means a pen the animal can hop in, with a solid floor, not a wire grid under a heavy body. Hay, pellets, and vegetables are the diet, and hay is the part that has to stay in front of the rabbit all day. Pellets and a handful of greens do not replace grass hay. A French lop that fills up on pellets gets heavier than the 10-15 lbs the breed already carries, and extra weight is the problem the feet are already solving. Nail trims belong in the same routine. Long nails change how a heavy rabbit stands, and then the hocks take the load at the wrong angle.",
    "Check the ears on a schedule, not only when the coat looks fine. A sour smell, head shaking, or a canal packed with debris is a reason to see a veterinarian who treats rabbits. Lop ears do not air the way an upright ear does. When you lift a French lop, support the chest and the hindquarters together. A rabbit this heavy that kicks while it dangles can break its own back. The moderate activity line still includes time out of the pen on a floor that does not slide. A large enclosure that is never opened is still a cage, and a 15 lb animal feels that sooner than a dwarf does.",
    "Keep the pen cool and out of direct sun. A dense, heavy rabbit handles heat poorly, and a French lop left in a hot room will not pant its way clear of that. Social, on this card, means a compatible rabbit if you house them together, introduced with room to separate. It does not mean a guinea pig as a roommate. A guinea pig is a different animal, needs vitamin C, and can be injured by a 10-15 lb rabbit sitting on it. Do not pair a French lop with a dwarf lop and call the sizes matched. The weight gap is the breed.",
    "Five to 7 years, written on the card as 5-7 years, is the whole lifespan to plan for. Hay, nail trims, ear checks, and a veterinarian who will see rabbits have to last that span, not a single season. A French lop that stops eating or stops leaving droppings needs that veterinarian the same day. A heavy lop does not get extra hours because the breed looks calm. The intermediate rating is the ears, the weight, the space, and the nails. It is not a coat that has to be clipped, and it is not the care sheet for a dwarf lop.",
  ],

  "small-animals/giant-african-land-snail": [
    "In the United States, the giant African land snail is federally regulated. The animal on this page is Lissachatina fulica, often sold under the older name Achatina fulica. Import, interstate movement, and possession are illegal without a USDA permit because this snail is a major crop pest. A shop label, a care card, or a low price does not replace that permit. This page is not a guide to obtaining one, hiding one, or shipping one. The legal status comes before any husbandry.",
    "The card lists an invertebrate of 6-8 inches, a lifespan of 5-10 years, a docile and low-maintenance temperament, beginner difficulty, and an origin in East Africa. Those lines describe the animal where keeping it is legal. They do not authorize possession in the United States. Beginner and low-maintenance are husbandry words. They are not a reading of federal pest law. A snail that can reach 6-8 inches and live 5-10 years is a long-lived crop pest, which is why the permit exists.",
    "The cost table on the card is initial setup $100-$500+, animal purchase $10-$500+, annual food and bedding $150-$500, and annual vet care $50-$200. Print those figures as the card's budget, not as a shopping list. A purchase price in the $10-$500+ range does not make an unpermitted snail legal to buy, to move across a state line, or to bring into the country. Spending the setup money on a secure enclosure is irrelevant if possession itself is prohibited where you live.",
    "Where these snails are legal to keep, the husbandry that actually matters is calcium, humidity, and a secure lid. The shell is built from calcium, and a snail kept short of it grows a thin, pitted shell even if the vegetable bowl looks full. They need the air in the enclosure damp, not a dry box with a water dish in one corner. They are strong for an animal that looks slow, and a loose lid or a gap at the rim is an escape. A 6-8 inch snail on the floor of a house is already a containment failure.",
    "They reproduce quickly. A legal keeper who starts with one or two can end up with a clutch, and then with far more snails than the original enclosure was built for, inside that same 5-10 year lifespan. Eggs should not be released outdoors. Do not tip substrate, egg clutches, or unwanted snails into a garden, a compost pile, or a ditch. That is how a crop pest leaves a terrarium. Surplus animals are a containment problem, not a gift to the yard.",
    "Humidity without a tight lid just makes an easier escape into a damp room. Calcium belongs in the diet the whole life, not as a one-time supplement when the shell already looks rough. A snail that seals itself to the glass for a long stretch may be resting, or it may be dry. Check the lid and the moisture before you decide the animal is fine because the card calls the temperament docile. Docile does not mean the animal stays put or that the shell maintains itself.",
    "Read the law that applies where you are before you read the beginner rating again. In the United States the controlling facts are the USDA permit and the ban on import, interstate movement, and possession without it. Elsewhere, the controlling facts are calcium, humidity, a secure lid, fast reproduction, and eggs that stay out of the wild. The 6-8 inch size and the 5-10 year lifespan are the animal. They are not permission.",
  ],

  "small-animals/hairless-rat": [
    "A hairless rat is a fancy rat variety, either patchwork or true hairless, and it is not a different species. The body under the missing coat is the same animal as a standard fancy rat. Size on the card is small-medium, 0.5-1.5 lbs. Lifespan is 1.5-3 years, which is the range to use for this variety, not a longer figure borrowed from another pet. Care level is intermediate. The extra work is skin and heat, not a new biology.",
    "Space is listed as 2 cu ft per rat, warm. The warm half of that line is the point of the hairless coat. These rats do not have fur to hold heat, so a cage that is fine for a coated rat in a cool room can leave a hairless rat chilled. Drafts, a cage against an outside wall, and a thin layer of bedding are the usual mistakes. They still need the 2 cu ft per rat, and they still need places to hide. Warmth is an addition to that space, not a substitute for it.",
    "Skin care is the other half of the variety. Hairless skin dries out, and it also collects oils and debris that fur would have carried away. A rat with flaky, greasy, or scabbed skin needs the cage kept clean and the skin looked at, not a coat-growing supplement and not a guess that the animal is supposed to look raw. Patchwork rats still have some fur and still have bare skin that can chap. True hairless rats have more of that skin exposed. Neither pattern is a separate species with a separate disease list.",
    "Respiratory disease and tumors are the same risks other rats carry. Mycoplasma-related respiratory disease is common in fancy rats, hairless or not. Mammary tumors are common in females of this species, and a hairless coat does not grant an exemption. A rat that wheezes, loses weight, or grows a lump needs a veterinarian who sees rats. The 1.5-3 year lifespan is already short. It gets shorter when those two problems are treated as quirks of a bald pet.",
    "Social needs are highly social, in pairs or groups, and activity is high. A hairless rat still wants company of its own kind and still wants time out and things to climb. The diet is lab blocks and fresh food, the same plan as a coated fancy rat. Do not house one alone because the skin looks fragile, and do not put it in with a mouse because both are small. Intermediate care means warmth, skin, the 2 cu ft, and the ordinary rat diseases. It does not mean a different animal.",
  ],

  "small-animals/harlequin-rabbit": [
    "The harlequin is a domestic rabbit breed with the harlequin pattern, either Japanese or magpie, and it is not a wild rabbit. Japanese harlequins split color down the face and the body. Magpie harlequins put a dark pattern on a white ground. Both are coat patterns on a barn-and-show breed, not a cottontail that was picked up outdoors. Size on the card is medium, 6.5-9.5 lbs. Lifespan is 5-8 years. Treating this rabbit as wildlife, or as an animal that should be turned loose, is a misreading of the breed.",
    "Care level is beginner, activity is moderate, and social needs are social. Beginner here means a rabbit that can be kept without a specialist coat routine. It does not mean a wild animal that will fend for itself in a yard. The 6.5-9.5 lb body still needs a pen, hay, and a veterinarian who sees rabbits. A harlequin that lives 5-8 years will be in that pen for the whole span. The pattern does not shorten the job and it does not turn the rabbit into a release candidate.",
    "Space is a 4x2 ft minimum. That footprint is the floor, not a suggestion to stack the rabbit in a small carrier between exercise days. A medium rabbit of 6.5-9.5 lbs uses a 4x2 ft pen as a starting size and still needs time out to hop. The floor should be solid. Wire under a rabbit this size is a sore-hock problem waiting on the pattern to distract you. Social housing means a compatible rabbit, introduced carefully, not a catch of local wild rabbits kept in the same hutch.",
    "The diet is hay, pellets, and vegetables. Hay is the main feed for the whole 5-8 years, because the teeth never stop growing and the gut slows down when the fiber stops. Pellets are a measured side dish. Vegetables are greens, not a bowl of fruit. A harlequin that drops food, drools, or grinds its teeth is a dental case, and the split-color face does not make the molars easier to see. Weigh the rabbit against the 6.5-9.5 lb range if the belly is rounding out while the hay rack stays full.",
    "Moderate activity still means a daily chance to move outside the 4x2 ft minimum. A patterned coat is not enrichment. Japanese and magpie are looks. The animal underneath is a domestic rabbit with the same need for hay, space, and a secure pen that any medium breed has. Do not keep one on a balcony you would not trust a pet rabbit on, and do not assume a wild rabbit's burrow is a model for this pen. The lifespan on the card is 5-8 years of that ordinary rabbit work.",
  ],

  "small-animals/hissing-cockroach": [
    "The Madagascar hissing cockroach is Gromphadorhina portentosa, a wingless insect from Madagascar. Size on the card is 2-3 inches. Lifespan is 2-5 years. They do not fly. The adults you buy are the large, heavy-bodied roaches, and they stay on the ground and on the bark you give them. A hissing cockroach in a room is an escape, not a moth that took off. The card rates them docile and hardy, at beginner difficulty, which is about a contained colony, not about an insect that can be left loose.",
    "Males carry bumps, the horns, on the pronotum, the shield behind the head. Those horns are how you tell a mature male from a female, and males use them when they shove each other. Females lack that pair of bumps. The hiss is not a rattle and not a warning from a mouthpart. They hiss by forcing air through the spiracles along the body. A sudden hiss when you open the lid is that air, from an animal that cannot fly away. Nymphs are smaller copies of the adults, not a different pest species that appeared in the bin.",
    "They want warmth and humidity, and they want a tight lid. A dry, cool tank slows them and cracks the conditions the card's hardy label assumes. Ventilation matters, but a screen with a gap, a lid that sits askew, or a cable hole is enough for a wingless roach that climbs. They do not need height for flight because they have no wings to use. They do need the lid latched. A colony of 2-3 inch adults on a kitchen floor is the failure the tight lid is there to prevent.",
    "Nymphs hatch as small versions of the same animal and grow through molts until they reach that 2-3 inch adult size. A tiny roach in the colony is not a German cockroach infestation and not a sign that a pest species got in, as long as it matches the hissing cockroach shape. Do not spray the enclosure with a household bug killer because you saw small ones. You will kill the animals you meant to keep. If a roach of a different shape shows up in the house, that is a separate problem from the nymphs in the tank.",
    "The temperament line, docile and hardy, fits animals that tolerate being looked at and that breed in a simple setup. It does not mean children should squeeze a 2-3 inch roach, and it does not mean the colony can live in an open plastic bin. Males will still wrestle. A startled adult will still hiss through those spiracles. Beginner difficulty assumes you can hold a temperature and a lid. The origin is Madagascar. These are not the roaches that arrive in a grocery bag, and they should not be released into a building or a yard to prove the point.",
    "The card prices the hobby at initial setup $100-$500+, animal purchase $10-$500+, annual food and bedding $150-$500, and annual vet care $50-$200. A hissing cockroach eats modestly, so the annual food figure is a ceiling for a careful keeper, not a target to hit with random scraps. They take vegetable matter and a little protein. A diet of only sweets, or a wet mess left to rot against the glass, is how a hardy animal ends up in foul air. Vet care at $50-$200 is the card's allowance. Finding someone who will see an invertebrate still has to happen before you need them.",
    "Plan on 2-5 years, the lifespan on the card, for a colony rather than for a single week of novelty. Adults are wingless. Males have the pronotum horns. The hiss is forced air through the spiracles. Nymphs are the same species, smaller. Warmth, humidity, and a tight lid are the enclosure. Docile and hardy are fair only inside that box. A hissing cockroach is not a pest cockroach you are raising on purpose, and it is not an insect that flies when the lid fails.",
  ],

  "small-animals/jersey-wooly": [
    "The Jersey wooly is a small wool rabbit breed from the United States, and it is not a guinea pig. Size on the card is 2.5-3.5 lbs. Lifespan is 7-10 years. Temperament is gentle and docile. Difficulty is listed as beginner. The coat is wool, which is the part those labels leave for you to discover after the rabbit is home. A guinea pig of a similar weight is a different animal with a different diet. This one is a rabbit, and the wool does not change that.",
    "Beginner, gentle, and docile describe the handling, not the coat. A Jersey wooly still has to be groomed. The wool mats, picks up bedding, and gets swallowed during a bath the rabbit gives itself. Swallowed wool is wool block. A mass of coat in the gut is a reason the rabbit stops eating, and a wool rabbit that is behind on grooming is the one that grows that mass. Brushing and removing loose wool is maintenance on a schedule, for the whole 7-10 years, not a show-day extra.",
    "Hay comes first. The diet of a Jersey wooly is the diet of a rabbit. Unlimited grass hay is the staple. Pellets are measured. Vegetables are a side, not the meal. Wool is not a reason to switch the animal to a guinea pig pellet or to a vitamin C supplement meant for a cavy. A rabbit that lives on soft food and treats and only nibbles hay is the rabbit whose gut slows. The wool makes that worse if the rabbit is also eating its coat. Hay is still the first thing in the pen each day.",
    "A rabbit that stops eating needs a vet the same day. That rule is not softer because the card says beginner, and it is not slower because the temperament says docile. A Jersey wooly that refuses hay, sits hunched, or produces no droppings may be in wool block or in gut stasis, and small rabbits go downhill in hours. Do not wait overnight because the breed is only 2.5-3.5 lbs and looks quiet. Quiet is the problem. The same-day visit is the response the size demands.",
    "The cost lines are initial setup $100-$500+, animal purchase $10-$500+, annual food and bedding $150-$500, and annual vet care $50-$200. Read the annual vet line against a 7-10 year life, not against a single checkup. Wool block and dental work blow past a $50-$200 year when they happen. The food and bedding range has to cover unlimited hay for a decade, plus the grooming tools the wool requires. A $10-$500+ purchase price is the rabbit. It is not the grooming and it is not the hay.",
    "House a Jersey wooly as a rabbit. The pen has to let a 2.5-3.5 lb animal hop, with a solid floor and hay in reach. Wool holds heat. A dense coat in a hot room is a risk even though the body is small. Do not shave the rabbit as a summer shortcut and then skip the hay because it looks less like a wool breed. Do not house it with a guinea pig. The guinea pig can be sat on, bullied, or fed the wrong pellet, and it still will not be company of the right species.",
    "Seven to 10 years, the card's 7-10 year lifespan, is a long time to keep a coat you chose because it looked soft. Gentle and docile are real. They do not cancel grooming, wool block, or the rule that a rabbit off its hay is a same-day patient. The animal is a small wool rabbit from the United States, 2.5-3.5 lbs, not a guinea pig and not a pet that can live on pellets. Hay first, then the brush, then the vet if the appetite stops.",
  ],

  "small-animals/long-haired-hamster": [
    "The long-haired hamster is the long-haired Syrian, the animal sold as a teddy bear hamster. The species is Mesocricetus auratus. It is not a dwarf hamster, and it is not a teddy guinea pig. Size on the card is 5-7 inches. Lifespan is 2-3 years. Origin is Syria. Temperament is gentle and friendly, and difficulty is beginner. Those words describe a single Syrian that is awake at night. They do not describe a pair of dwarfs, and they do not describe a cavy with a plush coat.",
    "A full-grown long-haired Syrian at 5-7 inches is a large hamster next to a dwarf species that stays near half that length. The cage has to match the Syrian, not the hamster aisle in general. Beginner care still means a large cage, not a colorful tube set sold as a starter kit. Tubes pinch a 5-7 inch body, hold urine, and are miserable to clean. The 2-3 year lifespan is short enough that a bad cage is a large share of the animal's life, not a temporary crate.",
    "Syrians are solitary. Adult long-haired hamsters fight if they are housed together. A pair that looked fine as juveniles will injure each other once they mature, and the long coat hides bites until the fur is stuck together. Do not add a second Syrian, a dwarf hamster, or a mouse as company because the first one looks lonely in the afternoon. It is asleep. Company of another adult is the thing that gets a Syrian hurt. One hamster to an enclosure is the housing plan.",
    "The cage should be large, and the bedding should be deep. Syrians dig and tunnel. A sprinkle of shavings over a solid pan is not bedding. Give a depth they can disappear under, and a hide that stays put when they burrow against it. A wheel is mandatory for an animal this active at night, and it has to be a wheel without bars that catch hair. Wire and rungs tangle a long coat, pull at the skin, and can break a toe. A solid running surface large enough for a 5-7 inch body is the one that fits this coat.",
    "They are nocturnal. The gentle, friendly temperament shows up in the evening, not at noon when someone wakes the hamster to prove it likes people. A long-haired Syrian that is pulled out of a nest every afternoon bites, and then gets labeled mean. Handle in the waking hours, with the lights low enough that you are not dazzling an animal that just came out of a burrow. Children who can only visit in the daytime are visiting a sleeping animal. That is a scheduling fact, not a training failure.",
    "The coat is the only reason this Syrian is sold under a different name. Long hair mats with urine and with bedding if the nest is wet or the wheel is the wrong shape. A light comb of the worst tangles is enough. This is not a breed you clip into a haircut, and it is not a guinea pig teddy. Spots of bare skin from barbering or from a fight are medical, not a haircut you missed. Because adults fight, you cannot solve a mat by adding a cagemate to groom it.",
    "The card's costs are initial setup $100-$500+, animal purchase $10-$500+, annual food and bedding $150-$500, and annual vet care $50-$200. Spend the setup range on the large cage, the deep bedding, and the solid wheel, not on a tube kit. Annual bedding costs are real because deep substrate is thrown out, not shaken. The $50-$200 vet line is a short-life animal's allowance. A Syrian that stops eating, drools, or has wetness at the tail still needs a vet inside that 2-3 year window. Cheek pouches packed with food can look like a tumor. Know the difference before you panic, and still have the clinic picked out.",
    "Two to 3 years is the lifespan on the card. Plan on one Mesocricetus auratus, nocturnal, in a large cage with deep bedding and a wheel that does not catch the coat. Gentle and friendly are evening traits of a solitary Syrian from Syria, 5-7 inches long. They are not an invitation to house adults together, and they are not the care sheet for a dwarf hamster or for a teddy guinea pig. When the 2-3 years are up, they are up. The coat does not add a decade.",
  ],

  "small-animals/mini-rex": [
    "The mini rex is a small rex rabbit with a plush, velvet coat, recognized in the United States in 1984. The scientific name on the card is Oryctolagus cuniculus, the same species as other domestic rabbits. Size is small, 3-4.5 lbs, or 1.4-2 kg. Lifespan is 8-12 years. It is not a mini lop, and it is not a guinea pig. The ears stand in the rex shape rather than lopping, and the animal does not need vitamin C the way a cavy does. People buy the coat and then house the rabbit like a guinea pig. That is the mix-up this card is for.",
    "The coat does not need clipping. Rex fur is short and dense, and the mini rex wears that plush texture at 3-4.5 lbs. Brushing out a little loose hair is plenty. A haircut, a shave, or a lion cut borrowed from a wool rabbit ruins the coat and is not required to keep the rabbit comfortable. The rabbit still needs hay, space, and a solid floor. Wire under a rex lets the thin coat rub straight against the metal, and the hocks pay for it. Hay, vegetables, and pellets are the herbivore diet on the card. Hay is the staple for the whole 8-12 years, because the teeth and the gut do not care that the coat is velvet.",
    "Space is a minimum enclosure of 4x body length. Measure the rabbit you have, not a picture of a mini lop. A 3-4.5 lb mini rex is small next to a standard rex and still too long for a guinea pig cage sold as a starter hutch. Activity is moderate to high, and social needs are high. The card calls them very affectionate and the care level beginner-friendly. Affection is time out of the pen and a handler who supports the hindquarters. It is not a lap animal that can live in a carrier. Eight to 12 years, the 8-12 year lifespan, is a long commitment for a rabbit people mistake for a low-care guinea pig. The velvet does not get clipped. The hay, the solid floor, and the 4x body length pen stay.",
  ],

  "small-animals/mongolian-gerbil": [
    "The Mongolian gerbil is Meriones unguiculatus, a small desert rodent, not a dwarf hamster and not a mouse. Size on the card is very small, 2-4 oz. Lifespan is 3-5 years. Activity is high. Care level is beginner. The beginner label fits a pair that can dig, take a sand bath, and stay together. It does not fit a single gerbil in a shallow layer of bedding with a hamster wheel as the only plan. The 3-5 year lifespan is long enough to regret a cage that cannot hold a tunnel.",
    "They are social, and they do best as a same-sex pair introduced young. The card's social line says same-sex pairs for that reason. Two males, or two females, that grew up together usually hold. Adult strangers dropped into one tank often fight, and a gerbil that has just lost its partner is a poor candidate for a surprise roommate. Do not split a bonded pair because one person wanted a single pet, and do not add a new gerbil to a grieving one on the day of the death. A lone gerbil can live, and many of them do worse than the pair the card is built around.",
    "They dig, and the space line is 10+ gal with deep bedding. A 10-gallon tank is the bottom of that range, and only if the bedding is actually deep. An inch of shavings in a 10+ gal tank wastes the gallons. Gerbils tunnel, and the substrate should let them disappear. A wire hamster cage with a shallow pan is the wrong shape even when the floor area looks similar. The lid has to be secure. They chew plastic and they kick bedding, and a loose screen is how a 2-4 oz animal leaves the tank.",
    "Give them a sand bath. A dish of bathing sand, not water, is how a gerbil keeps the coat from going oily. They should not be bathed in water. A damp gerbil in a deep, warm burrow chills, and the coat picks up bedding while it dries. Change the sand when it clumps. The diet is gerbil food, hay, and vegetables. Hay gives them something to chew besides the cage furnishings. Seeds are a side, not the whole ration. A gerbil that is only eating sunflower seeds gets fat under a coat that still looks fine at 2-4 oz.",
    "Some lines are seizure-prone. A gerbil that suddenly stiffens, rolls, or seems absent may be having a seizure, and swinging, shaking, or startling the animal is the wrong response. Note it, keep the room quiet, and talk to a veterinarian if the episodes repeat or the gerbil does not come back to normal. Do not swing a gerbil by the tail, and do not pick one up by the tail. The skin on a gerbil tail can slip off if it is pulled. That injury is ugly, it is painful, and it often ends with the damaged tail removed. Scoop the body instead. High activity and a 3-5 year life still depend on a same-sex pair, deep bedding in a 10+ gal tank, a sand bath, and a tail you never use as a handle.",
  ],

  "small-animals/mouse": [
    "The mouse on this page is the fancy mouse, Mus musculus, and it is not a dwarf hamster. Size is very small, 0.5-1 oz. Lifespan is 1.5-3 years. That is a short life, and it is the range on the card, not a longer span borrowed from a gerbil or a rat. Care level is beginner. Activity is high. People buy a mouse because it is cheap and small, then keep it like a solitary dwarf hamster. The species, the grouping, and the lifespan are all different.",
    "Females can live in groups. A small group of females, introduced with enough space, is the social plan the card points at when it says social, females in groups. Watch for a mouse that is barbered bald or kept off the food. That group is not working. Adult males usually fight. Two adult males in one enclosure is a common way to find blood on the bedding. House males singly unless you already have a proven compatible group, and do not add a strange adult male to another male's cage. A male and a group of females is a breeding colony, not a fix for loneliness.",
    "Space is a minimum of 1 sq ft per mouse. That is floor, measured for each animal, not a single square foot for a group of four. A high activity level in less than 1 sq ft per mouse turns into pacing and fighting. They need a wheel, bedding deep enough to dig in, and a secure lid. Mice climb and squeeze. A lid that rests on the rim without clips is an exit. The wheel should be solid enough that a tail and a foot do not drop through a gap. Bedding that is only a thin pad wastes the one behavior, burrowing, that a mouse does every night.",
    "The diet is mouse food, seeds, and vegetables. A lab block or a pelleted mouse diet should be the base, with seeds as a part of the mix rather than the entire bowl. Vegetables are small amounts. A 0.5-1 oz animal does not need a salad the size of a rabbit's. Fresh water has to be reachable. A bottle that looks full and is actually stuck is a common way to lose a mouse that was fine the day before. Check that the ball moves, because 1.5-3 years leaves little time to notice a slow dehydration.",
    "One and a half to 3 years, the card's 1.5-3 years, means the mouse you bring home is old before a dog would be out of puppyhood. Beginner care is a secure lid, a wheel, bedding, the 1 sq ft per mouse, and a group only when the sexes allow it. It is not a dwarf hamster's solitary setup with the name crossed out. Females together, adult males usually apart, Mus musculus rather than a hamster species. A mouse that stops eating or breathes with effort needs a veterinarian quickly. At this size, waiting a weekend is a large piece of a short life.",
  ],

  "small-animals/new-zealand-white": [
    "The New Zealand white is a large white domestic rabbit breed, and it is not a wild rabbit. Size on the card is large, 9-12 lbs. Lifespan is 5-8 years. The eyes are red. That red eye is the albino trait that comes with the white coat, not a sign of illness by itself and not a mark of a wild cottontail. Wild rabbits are a different life, a different color pattern, and not this breed. A New Zealand white in a backyard hutch is still a domestic animal that depends on hay and a pen.",
    "This breed is used in meat lines and in laboratory lines, and it is also kept as a pet. Those three roles are the same body. A rabbit from a meat or lab background does not arrive already suited to a small cage, and a pet-bred New Zealand white does not shrink to a dwarf because someone wanted a house rabbit. Nine to 12 pounds is the size on the card either way. If you want a pet, plan for a 9-12 lb animal with red eyes that will live 5-8 years, not for a cartoon of a laboratory rabbit that stays in a carrier.",
    "The diet is hay, pellets, and vegetables. Hay is the staple for a rabbit this large. A 9-12 lb body that lives on pellets gets heavy, and the extra weight sits on feet that are already carrying a large frame. Space is a 4x2 ft minimum. That is the floor the card allows at the bottom, and a large rabbit will use all of it. More length is better. A solid floor matters. Wire under a white rabbit of this weight is how hocks go bare. Social needs are social. A compatible rabbit can share space if they are introduced properly. A wild rabbit cannot.",
    "The dense white coat is a heat problem in sun. Albino skin under white fur burns, and a large rabbit in a sunny run overheats without a sign that looks like a color change. Shade, airflow, and a cool room are part of keeping a New Zealand white, especially in summer. Do not turn one out on a lawn at midday because the breed looks sturdy. Red eyes are also sensitive to glare. A hutch in full sun fails both the coat and the eye. The moderate activity line still means exercise in a place that is not an open oven.",
    "Care level is beginner and activity is moderate, which describes a calm large breed, not a rabbit that can be left in a 4x2 ft pen with no hay and no shade for 5-8 years. Beginner means the diet and the handling are straightforward if the space is real. Support the hindquarters when you lift 9-12 lbs. A New Zealand white is not a wild rabbit you rehab and release, and it is not a small pet that happens to be white. Red eyes, a dense white coat, hay, and a pen at least 4x2 ft are the card. Heat in the sun is the part the white coat adds.",
  ],

  "small-animals/peruvian-guinea-pig": [
    "The Peruvian guinea pig is a long-haired Cavia porcellus whose coat falls forward over the face. That direction is the breed. A silkie, also called a sheltie, grows long hair that sweeps back off the face. They are not the same haircut, and they are not interchangeable because both look fluffy in a photograph. Origin on the card is South America by selective breeding. Size is medium, 1.5-3 lbs, or 0.7-1.4 kg. Lifespan is 5-7 years. The hair over the eyes is the daily problem inside that lifespan.",
    "The coat needs care every day. If it is missed, it mats, and it covers the eyes. A Peruvian that cannot see its food will stop moving around the pen and will look dull for a reason that is mechanical, not mysterious. Show animals are often kept in wraps so the hair stays clean and out of the way until it is let down. A pet at home does not have to live in wraps, but then someone has to comb the hair, keep it off the face, and deal with mats before they tighten against the skin. Bathing a matted coat without combing it first makes felt. Care level is advanced because of that grooming, not because the animal is fierce.",
    "The diet is an herbivore diet: hay, vegetables, pellets, and vitamin C. The long hair does not change the vitamin C requirement. Guinea pigs do not make their own, and a Peruvian on hay alone with no vitamin C source will decline the same way a short-haired cavy does. Hay still has to be available all the time. Pellets made for guinea pigs, plus vegetables, are how most homes cover the vitamin C without turning the bowl into fruit. A coat full of sticky fruit juice is a mat waiting to happen. Activity is moderate. They will not exercise their way out of a coat that blinds them.",
    "Social needs are very high. They need companions. Space is a minimum of 7.5 sq ft for one and 10.5 sq ft for two. A Peruvian in a small pet-store cage, alone, with hair in its eyes, is outside every line on this card at once. Two animals need the 10.5 sq ft, and they need the grooming done on both, because a cagemate will not comb a Peruvian's face. Five to 7 years, the 5-7 year lifespan, is a long time to keep hair that grows toward the eyes. Advanced care is the comb, the vitamin C, the hay, and enough floor. It is not the care sheet for a silkie, whose hair sweeps the other way.",
  ],

  "small-animals/polish-rabbit": [
    "The Polish rabbit on this page is the small, short-faced US breed. It is not a Polish chicken, and it is not a person. Size is small, 2.5-3.5 lbs. Lifespan is 5-6 years. People search the name and land on poultry or on a demonym. This card is a rabbit, a compact domestic breed with a short face and upright ears. It is not a lop, and it is not a large white meat breed. The 2.5-3.5 lb body is the one the rest of the card is about.",
    "Care level is beginner, activity is moderate, and social needs are social. Beginner does not mean a rabbit that can share a room with a dog. A Polish is easy to injure if a dog shares the room. Four pounds of rabbit, at the top of the 2.5-3.5 lb range, is prey-sized to a dog that has never hurt a pet before. Curiosity is enough. A paw, a grab, or a shake ends a 5-6 year lifespan in a second. The pen has to be secure, with a lid or a height the dog cannot reach into, in a room the dog does not enter unsupervised.",
    "The diet is hay, pellets, and vegetables, and hay is the staple. A short face still hides continuously growing teeth. A Polish that drools, drops pellets, or prefers only soft food needs a dental exam, not a mashed diet as a permanent fix. The short face is a breed trait, not a defect you feed around until the molars overgrow. Weigh the rabbit. A 2.5-3.5 lb animal gets fat quickly on unlimited pellets, and extra weight on a small frame shows up as a reluctance to hop. Hay first, pellets measured, greens as the vegetable portion.",
    "Space is a 4x2 ft minimum. That footprint looks generous next to a 2.5-3.5 lb body, and it is still the minimum, not a luxury. Small rabbits are quick. A pen that is only as long as a carrier teaches them to spin in place. The floor should be solid, and the pen should close. A secure pen is the husbandry that matches the dog risk and the escape risk together. Social needs can be met by a compatible rabbit in that space. They are not met by a dog lying outside the bars as a guardian.",
    "Five to 6 years, the card's 5-6 year lifespan, is the commitment. It is shorter than several other small rabbit breeds, so do not stretch it in your head to a decade. Moderate activity means daily time out in a room you have actually checked. Cords, gaps behind furniture, and a dog door are the hazards that fit this size. Beginner care is hay, a 4x2 ft pen that latches, and a house rule that the dog is not in the room. The Polish rabbit is the US short-faced breed. It is not a chicken, not a nationality, and not a rabbit that can live at a dog's feet.",
  ],

  "small-animals/rat": [
    "A pet rat is a fancy rat, Rattus norvegicus, and it is not a mouse. Size is small-medium, 0.5-1.5 lbs. Lifespan is 2-3 years. That short life is the honest span on the card. Rats are social and intelligent, and they are short-lived, which is why a single season of neglect uses up a large share of the animal. Care level is beginner. Activity is high. Beginner means they settle in a house and learn routines. It does not mean they are a mouse that got big, and it does not mean they can live alone in a small cage.",
    "Social needs are highly social, in pairs or groups. They need friends of the same species. A rat kept singly, with human visits as the only contact, is under what this card is describing. House same-sex groups, or a neutered mix you can actually supervise. Do not add a mouse, a hamster, or a gerbil as the friend. Those pairings end with a dead smaller animal. Space is 2 cu ft per rat minimum. A pair needs twice that, plus height to climb. They also need time out of the cage, in a room that is proofed against cords and gaps. A large cage they never leave is still short of the way rats are kept well.",
    "Respiratory disease is common. Mycoplasma is part of why so many fancy rats sneeze, honk, or breathe with a rasp as they age. A little noise in a bright, active rat is something to watch. A rat that struggles to breathe, stops eating, or fades across a week needs a veterinarian who will treat rats. Pet-shop drops are not a plan, and this page does not set a drug dose. The 2-3 year lifespan is where this disease shows up. A rat that is wet around the eyes and nose with red staining, and that is also lethargic, is not just dusty.",
    "Mammary tumors are common in females. A lump on the belly, in the armpit, or along the underside can grow fast on a 0.5-1.5 lb body. Males get tumors less often and still get them. Finding a lump early is the reason to handle the rat often enough to know the skin. A veterinarian can tell you whether removal makes sense for that animal's age inside a 2-3 year life. Waiting until the mass is larger than the rat is how a common problem becomes an impossible one. Females are the group to watch most closely. The risk is not a reason to keep a female alone.",
    "The diet is lab blocks and fresh food. A formulated block should be the base, with vegetables and a little protein beside it, not a diet of crackers and leftovers. High activity needs something to climb, a solid wheel or a rope that cannot catch a foot, and that daily time out. Two to 3 years is the card's lifespan. Spend it on a pair or a group, 2 cu ft per rat at a minimum, and a cage that is actually large. Intelligent and social does not make a rat a mouse, and it does not make the respiratory disease or the mammary tumors optional.",
  ],

  "small-animals/rex-guinea-pig": [
    "A rex guinea pig is a guinea pig with a short, dense coat produced by a rex gene, and it is not a rex rabbit. The coat has rosettes, the swirls where the hair changes direction. Size on the card is 1.5-2.5 lbs. Lifespan is 5-7 years. Origin is South America. Temperament is friendly and social. Difficulty is beginner. The rabbit called a rex is a different species with a velvet coat and no vitamin C requirement. A rex guinea pig is still Cavia porcellus. The gene changed the hair. It did not turn the animal into a rabbit.",
    "Because the coat is short and dense, the skin is more exposed than on a long-haired cavy. Rosettes part the hair and leave little windows of skin. Wire floors, rough edges, and strong sun reach that skin faster than they reach a coated breed that hides it. Look at the rosettes when you handle the pig, not only at the back. Bald patches, scabs, or a sore under a swirl are easy to miss if you only pet the plush direction of the coat. Beginner care includes that look. The coat does not need the daily combing a Peruvian needs, and it still needs eyes on the skin.",
    "The diet is the guinea pig diet, whatever the rex gene did to the hair. They need vitamin C because guinea pigs do not make it. Hay is the staple that keeps the teeth and the gut moving. Pellets made for guinea pigs, plus vegetables, are the usual way to cover vitamin C without inventing a dose in this paragraph. A rex rabbit pellet, a rabbit mix, or a vitamin C plan copied from a rabbit will not do. Fruit is a treat. A sticky coat on a short rex is less dramatic than on a longhair, and the missing vitamin C is just as serious. Five to 7 years is a long time to feed the wrong species' food.",
    "Friendly and social means they want company of their own kind. A single rex guinea pig in a cage next to a rex rabbit is not a social life. Pair them with another guinea pig, with enough floor that the two can pass each other. Activity is not listed as a separate high-energy job on this card. They still move, popcorn, and use the space you give them. A carrier-sized cage is a poor match for a 1.5-2.5 lb animal you intend to keep 5-7 years. The short coat is not a reason to shrink the pen.",
    "South America, on the card, is the origin of the guinea pig, not a climate you have to recreate in the living room. They handle ordinary household temperatures and suffer in heat. A rex coat does not add the heat tolerance people imagine when they hear short hair. Shade, airflow, and water still matter on a hot day. The dense coat can also hide a wet belly if the bedding is soaked. Friendly handling should include a hand under the belly so you notice damp skin before it scalds.",
    "Costs on the card are initial setup $100-$500+, animal purchase $10-$500+, annual food and bedding $150-$500, and annual vet care $50-$200. The annual food range has to buy hay for 5-7 years, not a sack of mixed seed. Vitamin C is in the diet, not in a once-a-year purchase. The vet range is a planning number. A guinea pig that stops eating still needs a clinic that will see cavies, and that visit does not wait because the difficulty line says beginner. Setup money is the pen and the hay storage. It is not a rabbit hutch with a wire floor.",
    "Read the animal as a short-coated guinea pig with rosettes. The rex gene made the coat dense and left the skin easier to see and easier to injure. It did not make a rex rabbit, and it did not cancel vitamin C or hay. Friendly and social, beginner, South America, 1.5-2.5 lbs, 5-7 years: those are the card. The welfare additions are the exposed skin, a solid floor, and a companion of the same species. Pet the rosettes backward once in a while so you are looking at skin, not only at plush.",
  ],

  "small-animals/rex-rabbit": [
    "The rex rabbit on this page is the plush, short-coated rabbit, including the standard rex, which is larger than a mini rex. Size is medium, 7.5-10.5 lbs. Lifespan is 5-6 years. A mini rex is the small breed at a few pounds and a longer listed life. Do not use that other card's weight or its 8-12 year line here. This rabbit is the bigger velvet one. Guard hairs are reduced, so the coat feels like velvet rather than like ordinary rabbit fur with a soft undercoat. That missing guard hair is the breed and the reason the coat is thin.",
    "The coat does not need clipping. There is no wool to block the gut and no mane to wrap. What the coat needs is a floor that does not abrade it. A solid floor is the housing detail that matches reduced guard hairs. Wire rubs the thin coat and then rubs the skin, and a 7.5-10.5 lb rabbit puts real weight on the hocks while it sits. Sore hocks on a rex start as bald pink spots and get worse if the rabbit is left on metal. Bedding or a resting mat on a solid surface is the fix. A carpet the rabbit can catch a nail in is not.",
    "Space is a 4x2 ft minimum. A medium rabbit of 7.5-10.5 lbs fills a 4x2 ft pen and still needs exercise beyond it. The standard rex is not a mini rex that will be content in a smaller box because both say rex. Activity is moderate. Social needs are social. A compatible rabbit can share the pen if they are introduced with room to separate. The velvet is not a reason to house the animal alone in a display cage. Five to 6 years, the lifespan on the card, is the length of that housing decision.",
    "The diet is hay, pellets, and vegetables. Hay is the main feed, the same as for any rabbit, because the gut and the teeth did not change when the guard hairs did. A rex that lives on pellets gets heavy inside the 7.5-10.5 lb range and then carries that weight on thinner coat and vulnerable hocks. Greens are the vegetable portion. Fruit is occasional. A rabbit that stops eating hay, or that stops eating entirely, needs a veterinarian the same day. The velvet coat will not tell you the gut has slowed. The droppings and the hay rack will.",
    "Care level is beginner. Beginner means the diet and the temperament are straightforward, not that the floor can be wire. Handle a rex with a hand under the hindquarters. The coat feels sturdy because it is dense, and it is still a thin coat over a medium-sized rabbit. Do not shave a rex for summer. There is nothing to shave that helps, and the guard hairs will not grow back into a normal rabbit coat. The animal is the standard-sized rex, 7.5-10.5 lbs, 5-6 years, velvet because the guard hairs are reduced. Hay, space, and a solid floor are the care that matches that coat.",
  ],

  "small-animals/short-tailed-opossum": [
    "The short-tailed opossum is Monodelphis domestica, a small solitary marsupial from South America. It is not a sugar glider, and it is not a Virginia opossum. Size on the card is 4-6 inches. Lifespan is 4-8 years. There is no gliding membrane, no colony, and no life in the rafters of a North American house. People meet the name opossum and picture the Virginia animal, or they meet a marsupial and picture a sugar glider. This card is the small, short-tailed species. The 4-6 inch body is the scale.",
    "Four to 8 years is a short life compared with a cat. The card's 4-8 year range is the one to plan on. Do not stretch it to a cat's lifespan because the animal is a mammal that can learn your routine. Difficulty is intermediate. A 4-6 inch marsupial that may only live to the low end of 4-8 years does not have many seasons in which to recover from the wrong diet or the wrong housing. Intermediate means you set the enclosure up correctly before the animal arrives, not after a month of experiments.",
    "They do not have a pouch. Nothing about this species matches the picture of a mother with a belly pocket full of young. Females of this species lack that pouch. If you wanted a marsupial you could watch carry joeys in a pocket, this is the wrong animal, and a sugar glider is a different wrong animal. Do not prod the belly looking for a pouch that the species does not have. Curiosity on the card is the animal's trait, not an invitation to examine it like a specimen.",
    "Temperament is curious and solitary. Solitary housing is the plan. One short-tailed opossum to an enclosure. They are not a colony pet, and they are not happier in a pair the way sugar gliders are. Housing two together because both are small marsupials is how animals get bitten and stressed. Curious means they investigate the cage and your hand. It does not mean they want to live in a pocket or sleep in a group. A hide, and enough room to move at night, matters more than a companion.",
    "They can bite. A 4-6 inch animal has a mouth that can break skin, and a solitary marsupial does not owe you handling. Intermediate care includes a plan for an animal that would rather be in the hide. Use a cup or a short tunnel to move one if it does not step onto a hand. Do not pull it out by the tail. Children who need a pet that tolerates grabbing are looking at the wrong card. Curious animals still bite when they are restrained. The short life does not make a bite harmless.",
    "The diet is insectivore and omnivore. Insects and other animal protein matter. A fruit cup meant for a sugar glider, or a seed mix meant for a hamster, is not this diet. Offer a varied menu built around that insectivore and omnivore need, and keep water available. Do not free-feed sweets because the animal is only 4-6 inches and seems to burn it off. Origin is South America. You do not need a rainforest room. You do need a secure lid, because a curious marsupial will leave through a gap a jar could not.",
    "Costs on the card are initial setup $100-$500+, animal purchase $10-$500+, annual food and bedding $150-$500, and annual vet care $50-$200. The setup range has to buy an enclosure for a solitary animal, with a hide and a lock, not a sugar-glider colony cage you already own. Annual food in the $150-$500 band is insects and the rest of an omnivore diet across 4-8 years, scaled to the year. Vet care at $50-$200 assumes you can find a clinician who will see a marsupial before you need one. Monodelphis domestica is the species. No pouch, no glider, no Virginia opossum, solitary housing, and a bite you should expect if you force the handling.",
  ],

  "small-animals/stick-insect": [
    "Stick insect on this page is a generic name, not one species with one diet. The card lists size as 3-12 inches, which is already the clue. A three-inch species and a twelve-inch species do not eat the same leaves, do not need the same height of cage, and do not live as the same pet. Lifespan is 1-3 years. Origin is worldwide tropical. The keeper has to know the species. A care sheet written for walking sticks in general will starve the animal you actually bought.",
    "Diet is species-specific. Bramble suits many of the common species. Privet suits others. Eucalyptus is the leaf for some of the large ones and a refusal for others. Those are examples of how far apart the menus are, not a buffet to offer all at once. Identify the insect, then feed the plant that species eats. A stick insect that ignores the greens in a mixed bowl is not being picky for temperament's sake. It cannot use the wrong leaf. Replace the food before it wilts, and keep the stems in water in a way the insects cannot drown in.",
    "The card calls them calm and gentle, at beginner difficulty. Calm is the posture of an animal that hides by looking like a twig. Gentle is not a request to be handled. They are not pets that enjoy handling. Legs detach. A grab around the body, a pull off the screen, or a drop onto a table can leave a leg behind. Some species can drop a leg on purpose when they are seized. Missing legs do not grow back on an adult the way a story about insects sometimes claims. Watch them in the enclosure. That is the interaction the calm label is describing.",
    "Many species are parthenogenetic. Females lay fertile eggs without a male. A single female in a tank is not a guarantee of one pet. She can fill the floor with eggs across the 1-3 year lifespan, and those eggs can take months to hatch, sometimes after you think the group is gone. Eggs look like seeds. They are small, hard, and easy to tip out with old leaf litter. Do not discard them outdoors where the species is not native. A pot of floor debris emptied into the garden is a release. Bag it and dispose of it as waste, or keep it if you meant to hatch it and you are allowed to.",
    "Some places regulate stick insects. The beginner line on the card is not a permit. Before you keep a species, especially one that can reproduce without a male and lay eggs that look like seeds, check the rules where you live. A tropical species that is ordinary in one country can be a restricted organism in another. This page will not invent a ban the card does not name. It will say the check is part of acquiring the animal, and that eggs and shed leaves do not belong in native habitat.",
    "Use a secure mesh lid. Stick insects climb, and they hang from the screen to molt. A solid lid with a few holes starves them of the grip they need, and a loose cloth cover is an exit. The cage has to be tall enough for the species you have, because a molt needs room to hang. A 3-inch nymph and a 12-inch adult are not the same height requirement. The card's 3-12 inch range is why you match the tank to the species name, not to the middle of the range. Humidity and airflow have to suit that species too. A soggy, sealed box molds the leaves. A desert tank dries out a species that needed moisture to molt.",
    "Costs are initial setup $100-$500+, animal purchase $10-$500+, annual food and bedding $150-$500, and annual vet care $50-$200. The annual food figure is the host plant, which may be bramble you collect or a plant you grow because privet and eucalyptus are not in the yard. Know which one before you count the $10-$500+ purchase as the whole expense. Vet care for a stick insect is limited. The $50-$200 line does not create a clinic. Beginner care, for 1-3 years, is the right leaf, a mesh lid, legs left alone, and eggs that never reach the garden. Calm and gentle stay inside the tank.",
  ],

  "small-animals/tarantula": [
    "Tarantula on this page is a generic name. The card does not name one species. Size is 3-10 inches of legspan, lifespan is 5-30 years, and origin is worldwide tropical and subtropical. That spread is the point. A spider at 3 inches and a spider at 10 inches, one that lives near 5 years and one that can live toward 30, are not the same pet. Do not collapse 5-30 years into a single age, and do not invent a species name this card does not give. Temperament is calm and docile, with the card itself adding that this is species-dependent. Difficulty is beginner-intermediate. Both labels depend on which spider is in the enclosure.",
    "New World species have urticating hairs and usually milder venom. Those hairs are a defense. The spider kicks them off the abdomen when it is disturbed, and they irritate skin, eyes, and lungs. A calm New World spider is still carrying that defense. Usually milder venom is not a promise that a bite is nothing. It is a comparison with the other half of this hobby. The hairs are the reason a New World tarantula is a poor animal to pass around, even when the species has a reputation for sitting still.",
    "Old World species lack those urticating hairs. They tend to be faster, and they have medically more significant venom. A bite from an Old World tarantula is a medical problem, not a badge and not a moment for a home remedy or a dose you found online. This page does not name a species and does not give a treatment. It says the keeper has to know which spider they bought before they even think about handling. Speed plus a more serious venom is a bad combination in an animal someone assumed was the calm one on a poster.",
    "Most tarantulas should not be handled. The calm, docile line is species-dependent, and it describes an animal left in its enclosure, not a spider that wants to be on a hand. Choosing to handle one starts with knowing whether you have a New World species with urticating hairs or an Old World species without them. If you do not know, you do not handle it. A display animal that never comes out is a complete life for a tarantula. Nothing in the 5-30 year span requires a handling session to be successful.",
    "They need a secure lid. Tarantulas climb, including species that spend their time on the ground, and they push. A lid that relies on the animal's weight, or a top that slides, is how a spider ends up on the floor. The enclosure also needs the right moisture for that species. Arboreal spiders need height, airflow, and a moisture level that does not turn the tank into a swamp. Fossorial spiders need depth they can dig and a moisture level that suits a burrow, which is not the same setting. One average humidity poured over every tarantula will be too wet for one species and too dry for another. The 3-10 inch legspan changes the floor space as well. Match the box to the spider you have.",
    "A fall onto a hard floor can rupture the abdomen. That is the practical reason the lid stays on and the spider stays in the enclosure. An abdomen that splits is an injury an invertebrate does not walk off. Handling over a table, a tile floor, or a sink is how that fall happens. Even a short drop is enough. The beginner-intermediate rating assumes the animal is contained. It does not assume the keeper can catch a falling spider safely. If a tarantula gets out, guide it with a cup and a piece of card back into the enclosure. Do not grab it.",
    "Costs are initial setup $100-$500+, animal purchase $10-$500+, annual food and bedding $150-$500, and annual vet care $50-$200. A tarantula eats infrequently compared with a mammal, so the annual food range is insects on a slow schedule, not a daily bowl. The purchase range is wide because the card is not one species. A cheap spider and an expensive spider can both sit inside $10-$500+, and the cheap one may be the Old World species you are not prepared to keep. Setup money is the locking lid, the right orientation of the tank, and the substrate depth. Across a lifespan that can run 5-30 years, the enclosure outlasts several other pets. Know which spider you bought. Most should not be handled. The lid stays shut.",
  ],

  "small-animals/teddy-guinea-pig": [
    "A teddy guinea pig is a short-coated Cavia porcellus with a dense, kinky coat and a plush texture. It is not long-haired, and it is not a teddy-bear hamster. The long-haired Syrian sold as a teddy bear is Mesocricetus auratus, a solitary rodent with a 2-3 year life. This card is the guinea pig. Origin is a spontaneous mutation in the 1960s-70s. Size is medium, 1.5-3 lbs, or 0.7-1.4 kg. Lifespan is 5-7 years. The coat can feel harsh or soft depending on the line. Neither feel is a different breed, and neither feel means the hair should be grown out or clipped into a style. Short, dense, and kinky is the whole coat description.",
    "The plush coat does not change the diet. Teddy guinea pigs are herbivores. They need hay, vegetables, pellets, and vitamin C. Guinea pigs do not make vitamin C, and a kinky coat is not a substitute. Hay is in the cage all the time so the teeth wear and the gut keeps moving. A teddy that looks round under a dense coat can be fat or can be hiding a dull appetite. Weigh against the 1.5-3 lb range rather than guessing through the plush. Activity is moderate. They will run and popcorn, and they will not exercise their way out of a missing vitamin. The harsh-coated lines and the soft-coated lines eat the same plan.",
    "Social needs are very high. They need companions of their own species, not a hamster and not a rabbit. Space is a minimum of 7.5 sq ft for one and 10.5 sq ft for two. A single teddy in a small cage is outside both the social line and the floor line. Care level is beginner-friendly, which fits a short coat that does not need the daily wrap-and-comb routine of a Peruvian. Beginner-friendly still includes vitamin C, hay, and the 7.5 sq ft. Part the kinky coat when you handle them. Harsh or soft, the density can hide a scab or a wet belly. Five to 7 years is the lifespan. The coat stays short. The vitamin C and the hay do not become optional because the animal feels like a stuffed toy, and the animal never becomes a teddy-bear hamster.",
  ],

  "small-animals/texel-guinea-pig": [
    "A texel guinea pig is a long-haired guinea pig with a curly coat. It is not a texel sheep, and it is not a silkie. The sheep is a livestock breed that shares a name. The silkie, or sheltie, is a guinea pig whose long hair is straighter and sweeps back off the face. The texel curls. Size on the card is medium, 1.5-2.5 lbs. Lifespan is 5-7 years. Care level is intermediate because of that coat. People who search the name and expect either a sheep or a smooth longhair will mismanage the hair in front of them.",
    "The curls mat. High grooming need is the whole intermediate rating. A texel that is combed once a week and otherwise left to look charming will felt. Mats pull on the skin, collect urine, and hide sores. The curl is tighter than a silkie's wave, so a routine copied from a silkie is often not enough. Work the coat down to the skin often enough that you never discover a mat by accident. Bathing a felted curl makes the mat worse. Cut the problem out only if you can do it without the skin, or have someone do it who has done a texel before. This is not a sheep shear on a schedule. It is a guinea pig that mats.",
    "The diet is hay, pellets, vegetables, and vitamin C. The curls do not change the fact that a guinea pig needs vitamin C and needs hay in the pen all day. Fruit juice in a curly coat is how mats start in earnest. Keep treats small and the hay constant. A texel at 1.5-2.5 lbs is easy to overfeed because the coat adds visual bulk. Weigh the animal. A dense coat over a thin body is the opposite problem, and you will not see it if you only look at the silhouette. Intermediate care is the comb and the scale, plus the ordinary cavy diet, for 5-7 years.",
    "Social needs are social, and the card says to keep them in pairs. Space is 7.5 sq ft minimum. That minimum is tight once two curly coats are sharing it, and a pair is what the social line is asking for. One texel alone in 7.5 sq ft meets the floor and misses the pair. Two texels need enough room to pass, and both need the grooming. A cagemate will not keep the curls open. Activity is moderate. They are not a high-drive animal whose coat you can ignore because they are busy. They are a medium guinea pig that sits in its own hair.",
    "Intermediate, not beginner, is the right reading of a long curly coat on a 1.5-2.5 lb cavy that lives 5-7 years. Hay, pellets, vegetables, and vitamin C stay in the diet no matter how much time the grooming takes. The name is not a sheep, and the coat is not a silkie. If you wanted a short-coated guinea pig, a texel is the wrong card. If you keep one, the curls are a daily material, the pair is the social plan, and 7.5 sq ft is only the floor the card is willing to call a minimum.",
  ],

  "small-animals/white-crested-guinea-pig": [
    "A white crested guinea pig is a short-coated guinea pig with a single white rosette crest on the head. The crest is the breed point. Body color varies. A white crest can sit on a dark body, a golden body, or a mixed one, and the rest of the coat is not supposed to be a long white skirt. Size is medium, 1.5-2.5 lbs. Lifespan is 5-7 years. Care level is beginner. People see the white on the head and assume the animal is an albino, a longhair, or a different species. It is a short-coated Cavia porcellus with one white rosette.",
    "The rosette is a swirl of white hair on the forehead, not a hat of fur you can ignore. A correct crest is single. Extra swirls on the body move the animal toward a different pattern and are a breed-show question, not a health plan. For a pet, the useful fact is simpler. The crest is hair over skin on the head, and it is the place keepers forget to look because the white patch is supposed to be there. Body color varying does not change the diet or the pen. A black-bodied white crest and a buff-bodied white crest eat the same hay.",
    "Part the crest when you check the animal. The white hair hides redness, flakes, mites, and a scratch that a short coat on the back would show immediately. Lift the rosette, look at the skin, and then let it fall back. A crest that is wet, sticky, or packed with bedding is not a style. It is a dirty patch on the head. Short coats are otherwise the low-grooming end of guinea pigs. This breed is that short coat plus one place that needs a deliberate look. Beginner care includes parting that crest. It does not include growing the crest out or tying it into a topknot.",
    "The diet is hay, pellets, vegetables, and vitamin C. The crest does not change the vitamin C need. Hay is available all the time. A white crested pig that stops eating is a same-day concern at 1.5-2.5 lbs, the same as any guinea pig, because the body is small and the lifespan of 5-7 years still drops quickly when the gut stops. Social needs are social, and the card says to keep them in pairs. Space is 7.5 sq ft minimum. A pair of short-coated pigs can share that floor if they get along, and both crests still get checked. Activity is moderate.",
    "Beginner is a fair rating for a short coat and a single breed point you can see from across the pen. It is not a rating for a long-haired cavy, and it is not a reason to skip vitamin C because the animal looks tidy. Body color will vary from pig to pig. The white that matters is the crest. Part it, feed hay, give the 7.5 sq ft, and keep a companion. Five to 7 years is the lifespan on the card. The rosette stays white. The skin under it is what you are actually inspecting.",
  ],
};
